    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8e452071-2b73-5bf2-8321-85cf182e6a66",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4=2\\left(\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)$\n- **II.** $x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4=2\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)$\n- **III.** $x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4=2\\left(e^{x}\\right)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4 & =2\\left(\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2\\left(\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)-4}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{2 \\left(\\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 2\\right)}{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4 & =2\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)-4}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{2 \\left(\\sqrt{x} - 2\\right)}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4 & =2\\left(e^{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2\\left(e^{x}\\right)-4}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{2 \\left(e^{x} - 2\\right)}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
